{"step_index":1143,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:37:18Z","thinking":"**Considering the Roman Weight Case**\n\nExamining the case where `romanWeight f` equals 3, a conflict arises. The prior proof established that if `romanWeight f` is less than or equal to 3, then `radius G` is at most 2. However, this contradicts the assumption since `radius G` would then be less than or equal to 2, implying that `radius G + 1 <= 3`. This seems to be the critical constraint to follow.\n\n\n**Generalizing Roman Weight Constraint**\n\nIf `romanWeight f` is less than or equal to 3, then it follows that `radius G + 1 <= romanWeight f`. Considering the case where `romanWeight f` is greater than or equal to 4 and assuming `radius G <= 3`, then `radius G + 1 <= 4 <= romanWeight f`, which holds. It appears `radius G <= romanWeight f - 1` consistently holds.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"sleep 6 && tail -n 2 /tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"7000","toolAction":"\"Checking conductor step 1174\"","toolSummary":"\"Tail conductor transcript\""}}]}
