{"step_index":1146,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:37:33Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:37:33+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:37:39+02:00\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\n{\"step_index\":1175,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T23:37:21Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Considering Roman Domination Number**\\n\\nThe goal is to prove an inequality involving the Roman domination number.  Starting by applying `le_romanDominationNumber` suggests a strategy involving considering an arbitrary Roman dominating function and bounding its weight.  The proof proceeds by casework on the cardinality of the vertex set relative to the function's weight.\\n\\nIf the cardinality is bounded, it uses a previously proven lemma. Otherwise, it must now consider the case when the cardinality is not bounded. Now proceeding by another case on graph radius.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Radius Bound and Weight**\\n\\nThe goal is to show `radius G + 1 <= romanWeight f`. A case split on `radius G <= 2` is proposed. If `radius G <= 2`, it can be shown that `radius G + 1 <= 3 <= romanWeight f` because the hypothesis `hw2` states `3 <= romanWeight f`. Thus, `omega` solves this case.\\n\\nIf `radius G <= 2` is false, then `3 <= radius G`.\\n\\n\\n**Bounding Roman Weight Based on Radius**\\n\\nSince `3 <= radius G` and `radius G + 1 <= card V`, it follows that `4 <= card V`. Attempting to prove `romanWeight f >= radius G + 1`. Analyzing `romanWeight f = 3` leads to a contradiction because it implies `radius G <= 2`. Now consider when `radius G = 3`, then `4 <= romanWeight f`.\\n\\nGeneralizing the argument to `radius G = 4` shows `5 <= romanWeight f`. The pattern suggests a proof can be formalized to remove case splits.\\n\\n\\n**Refining Roman Weight Bound**\\n\\nConsidering a contradiction proof, the agent attempts to prove that the Roman weight is greater than or equal to 4. Assuming `romanWeight f <= 3` leads to contradictions in both subcases. The initial case split on Roman weight less or equal to 3 has failed.\\n\\nIf `radius G <= 3`, the desired inequality holds directly. If `radius G >= 4`, based on the hypothesis `radius G + 1 <= Fintype.card V` means `5 <= Fintype.card V`. The agent is unsure if any real challenges would have this condition.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"search_web\",\"args\":{\"query\":\"\\\"\\\\\\\"rad(G) + 1 \\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\gamma_R\\\\\\\" OR \\\\\\\"radius(G) + 1 \\\\\\\\le \\\\\\\\gamma_R\\\\\\\" OR \\\\\\\"rad(G) + 1 <= \\\\\\\\gamma_R\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching for exact formula in literature\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search exact formula in literature\\\"\"}}]}\r\n{\"step_index\":1177,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T23:37:35Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"search_web\",\"args\":{\"query\":\"\\\"\\\\\\\"On Roman domination in graphs\\\\\\\" Cockayne radius\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching Cockayne paper for radius\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search Cockayne paper for radius\\\"\"}}]}\r\n\n"}
